## Trailer definitions in msil-ld (ImplementationPlan.md 5.14.4 T1 and
## T4-T6, 5.14.2's resolution order; M24.d (4) and (5); D36 as amended by
## decision brief 3.2). msil-ld adds one lazy synthetic input, '<trailer>',
## after the command-line inputs. It is searched like an archive, after every
## real archive and before native libraries, also for the references that
## code generation creates after LTO (helpers-switches E5), and it offers a
## name only when its scope holds for the output. M24's one definition, the
## scope Any row of TrailerDefs.def, is
##   assembly static void '__msil_compiler_barrier'() cil managed noinlining { ret }
## a MethodDef of '<Module>' with flags 0x0013 (assembly static), ImplFlags
## 0x0008 (NoInlining), '.maxstack 0' and a body of 'ret'. Every single-thread
## fence and every empty asm barrier compiles to a call of it as of an
## undefined function (a MemberRef on '<msil>Symbols' in the object).
## - A fence object binds both calls of main to that method: two fences on
##   windows-msvc; an empty asm statement with a memory clobber and a signal
##   fence on x86_64 Linux.
## - An object definition and an archive member win over '<trailer>', the
##   member also when a native library comes first on the command line; a
##   native export of the name loses to it (an import library and an ELF
##   shared object; N3 names never bind natively).
## - The map file names '<trailer>' as an input, as the symbol's input and as
##   the method's input; --trace-symbol and --why-extract name it as the
##   definition.
## - -shared defines it in '<Module>' and does not export it: the export
##   class lists only the library's own function, and a program linked
##   against the library has its own copy.
## - LTO with an image resolves the references that code generation creates
##   after LTO; --lto-emit-asm text defines the method (T6) and calls it.
## - A link without a barrier has no such method and no '<trailer>' input.

# REQUIRES: llvm-cil-dump
# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj fence.ll -o fence.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj asm.ll -o asm.o
